{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "label": "Recommended HTTP security stack", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "Ordering, outermost first: requestId (so every log line including errors has an id), securityHeaders (above everything that rejects, so those responses carry the headers), cors (outside auth - preflights are anonymous by spec), requestTimeout (bound latency before real work), handleErrors (outside csrf so it maps that middleware's own 403, inside the header layers so what it returns still flows through them), csrf, then fallback at the root.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_withsecurityheaders", "label": "withSecurityHeaders (server-wide)", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "nosniff is the only header on by default, deliberately: it is the only one of the bundle that cannot change how an existing application is embedded, framed or referred to, whereas X-Frame-Options and Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy would break iframes, cross-origin embedding and OAuth popups. Configuring the bundle on the server rather than the route tree reaches the middleware-invisible paths too.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_serializecookie", "label": "serializeCookie", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "Safe by omission: an attribute you do not mention resolves to the strict end (Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Path=/), so the two-argument call is already shippable and satisfies the __Host- rules without a word about them - loosening is the part you write down. Path is always emitted because otherwise the browser derives scope from the request URI. It refuses an illegal name, value, Path or Domain, since those reach the header verbatim and the last Domain is the one that counts, so an unchecked Path could override yours rather than lose to it.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_error_hygiene", "label": "Error hygiene", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "Return a generic body and log the detail server-side keyed by the request id; keep 403/404 unspecific. The server-side copy needs hygiene too - the usual leak is a connection URL, so redactUrlCredentials / redactedUrlLabel mask userinfo, because a password that has reached a log aggregator has to be rotated, not deleted.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}, {"id": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_input_caps", "label": "Capping untrusted input", "file_type": "rationale", "rationale": "Request bodies are capped at 1 MiB on every backend and refused with 413 before the handler runs and without consulting withErrorHandler, because a size cap is a transport decision rather than an application error; an over-cap Content-Length is refused before a byte is read, and a chunked body is counted as it arrives. WebSocket frames are capped by maxFrameBytes. Responses you fetch are untrusted input too - the direction easiest to forget - and carry a 30 s deadline and 8 MiB ceiling.", "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_middleware_request_id_requestid", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_middleware_security_headers_securityheaders", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_middleware_cors_cors", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_middleware_timeout_requesttimeout", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_error_handling_handleerrors", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_middleware_csrf_csrfprotection", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_recommended_stack", "target": "docs_src_content_docs_http_error_handling_fallback", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/src/content/docs/http/security.mdx", "source_location": null, "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_src_content_docs_http_security_r